  "tldr": "Scheduled inspections identify each arm's factual competing-event bin probabilities but erase the within-bin ordering needed to splice the cause-1 hazard from arm 1 with the cause-2 hazard from arm 0. The sharp component-risk interval is obtained by one-bin incidence-survival support envelopes and an exact backward recursion. The central open result asks whether every envelope extremum over bounded measurable hazards has a universal finite common-piece representation, with an explicit bound and an elementary finite optimization; matched mesh-width bounds and finite-sample multinomial inversion supply design and inference consequences without replacing that structural theorem.",
  "project_justification": {
